\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces An allosteric regulator (or allosteric modulator) is a substance that binds to a site on an enzyme or receptor \textit  {distinct from the active site}, resulting in a conformational change that alters the protein's activity, either enhancing or inhibiting its function. In contrast, substances that bind directly to an enzyme's active site or the binding site of the endogenous ligand of a receptor are called orthosteric regulators or modulators. A – Active site, B – Allosteric site, C – Substrate, D – Inhibitor, E – Enzyme. This is a diagram of allosteric regulation of an enzyme. (from \textit  {Wikipedia Eng}) }}{31}{figure.2.3}\protected@file@percent }
